* PW_COMPLEX(pw)
* Return @true if pw meets SD's password rule.
* Copyright (c) String Database
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Sep 26 dm  Written on the owner's ruling of 19 Sep 2026: SD requires a
*               complex password itself, whatever the operating system allows,
*               for SDSYS and every other account.  His choice of rule: at
*               least 8 characters, and all four kinds.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !pw_complex(pw)
*
*    THE RULE - one rule, every password SD sets (the SD password through
*    MODIFY.PASSWORD, the Linux password CREATE.ACCOUNT sets, and the sdsys
*    password the installer sets):
*
*      at least 8 characters, and at least one of each:
*        a lower-case letter   a-z
*        an upper-case letter  A-Z
*        a digit               0-9
*        a symbol              any other printable ASCII character,
*                              space included
*
*    Anything outside printable ASCII (a control character, a tab, a byte
*    above 126) fails the rule outright.  The classes are ASCII ranges on
*    purpose: the same rule is written again in bash (sd-elevate's
*    pw_complex, installsdai.sh's sdsys prompt) and in the Windows port, and
*    byte ranges are the one definition all of them read the same way.
*    Refusal text: message 10920, shared with SD Core for Windows.
*
*    It judges the password only; it says nothing and changes nothing.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function pw_complex(pw)
$catalog !pw_complex

   if len(pw) < 8 then return @false

   has.lower = @false ; has.upper = @false
   has.digit = @false ; has.symbol = @false

   n = len(pw)
   for i = 1 to n
      c = seq(pw[i,1])
      begin case
         case c >= 97 and c <= 122 ; has.lower = @true
         case c >= 65 and c <= 90  ; has.upper = @true
         case c >= 48 and c <= 57  ; has.digit = @true
         case c >= 32 and c <= 126 ; has.symbol = @true
         case 1 ; return @false       ;* not printable ASCII
      end case
   next i

   return (has.lower and has.upper and has.digit and has.symbol)

end

* END-CODE
